1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
And now, for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:10,500
Maybe you heard the name before.

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,160
Ruhl Amundsen, polar explorer.

4
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:20,080
He led the first Arctic expedition to reach the South Pole between 1910 and 1912.

5
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:25,280
He was also the first person to reach both the North and South Poles.

6
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:30,300
So this guy spent a fair amount of time outdoors, camping.

7
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:34,900
In pretty tough conditions, you would have to admit.

8
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:38,800
This is what he had to say about adventure.

9
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:44,780
He said, an adventure is merely a bit of bad planning brought to light by the test of trial.

10
00:00:45,940 --> 00:00:48,780
An adventure is merely a bit of bad planning.

11
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:54,560
Camping when the weather is clear and sunny, that's great camping.

12
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:57,820
It could be adventurous, but it's just great camping.

13
00:00:58,060 --> 00:00:59,960
Camping when the weather is...

14
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:01,000
interesting.

15
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:03,460
Now that's an adventure.

16
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:09,360
Hey, this is podcast number 44.

17
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:41,560
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast.

18
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:42,640
This is Clark Green.

19
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,860
Last time around, we spent a lot of the podcasts addressing an email that I received about patrols

20
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:52,560
and patrol leader elections.

21
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:54,900
And I got a couple of comments this week on it.

22
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:00,640
The first one comes from Larry Geiger, and he said, that was just spot on.

23
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:01,240
The whole thing.

24
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:02,039
Yes, yes, yes.

25
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:05,539
Maybe we should think of our troops as more parliamentarian.

26
00:02:06,039 --> 00:02:10,400
In other words, the Scouts form and reform their government or whatever you want to call it as needed.

27
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:13,740
I think that is how Baden-Powell would think of it.

28
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:19,060
Also, it was great that you said, we want things like this to happen.

29
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:19,960
That is so true.

30
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:26,140
And when we're talking about things like that, what Larry is talking about is, you know, there's a problem,

31
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:30,800
a difficulty, an inefficiency, a challenge.

32
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:32,680
We want those things to happen.

33
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:38,740
This attitude makes some folks very nervous, but it's exactly what we are trying to achieve.

34
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:43,700
Larry, quote, disciple of Clark, unquote, Geiger.

35
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:44,540
Oh, my.

36
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:45,360
All right.

37
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:48,880
Let's end the disciple thing.

38
00:02:51,220 --> 00:02:56,740
I also heard from Alan about the same subject.

39
00:02:56,740 --> 00:03:06,440
He said, in every troop I've been associated with, and that's six, I have always seen the practice of having a set term of office for troop youth leaders.

40
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:12,700
This applied to the senior patrol leader who's voted in by the whole troop and to patrol leaders who are voted in by the patrol.

41
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:16,500
The patrol leaders were considered troop leaders.

42
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:21,960
Every six months to a year, these troops would hold an official election to elect those youth leaders.

43
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:30,680
This is the standard of practice of every troop I've ever heard of, but you're saying that we should train our scouts on the patrol level to elect a new patrol leader whenever they want.

44
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:37,800
So if they elect a guy and then just two weeks later feel disappointed in the decision, they just do it again.

45
00:03:38,360 --> 00:03:39,960
This brings to mind a few questions.

46
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:43,240
Do we lay any ground rules or set up a process for this to happen?

47
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:47,500
If we think in terms of a parliamentary system, your idea seems fine.

48
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:59,400
But in our constitutional democracy, there is always an agreed upon process that is followed beforehand that sets the term of office, a candidate's qualifications, a chain of command, an official steps down, etc.

49
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:05,320
I know we should coach our scouts through the patrol building process, but it's very hard when you haven't seen it modeled very much.

50
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:09,180
Well, Alan, those are excellent thoughts.

51
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:13,040
And I got to say, these comments and questions keep me on my toes.

52
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:19,940
If, as I suggest, scouts can elect a patrol leader whenever they want, it doesn't immediately follow that they're going to do this impulsively every two weeks.

53
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:25,700
Well, I mean, while this could happen in theory, it's not my experience that it happens in practice.

54
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:38,800
Now, I'm not so much concerned that scouts are learning about types of government and governmental systems as they are about the underlying principles of the autonomy tempered by responsibility, and that's the heart of any democratic process.

55
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:48,120
In the situation that was described to me, it was clear that this no-show patrol leader needed to either start carrying his weight or to be replaced.

56
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:51,620
The question was how one replaces a patrol leader.

57
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:54,800
My answer was that the patrol elects a new leader.

58
00:04:55,740 --> 00:04:57,140
So the next question follows.

59
00:04:57,460 --> 00:04:58,500
How does this happen?

60
00:04:58,580 --> 00:05:01,540
My answer was that the patrol can elect a leader at any time.

61
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:04,360
This does not necessarily mean they do it cavalierly.

62
00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:09,340
There's a common false dichotomy, I think, when we're discussing youth leadership.

63
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:19,000
We think that if we give them full responsibility and authority, we're just going to stand by and watch the whole thing descend into chaos.

64
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:23,380
The other extreme is that we're going to make all the decisions and plans for them.

65
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:25,460
We're going to be coaching and mentoring them.

66
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:28,420
Our older scouts are going to be coaching and mentoring them.

67
00:05:28,780 --> 00:05:31,960
And that's a check on scouts making impulsive and poor decisions.

68
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:39,380
I think we need to invest our scouts with autonomy to make decisions, but this comes with the expectation of responsibility.

69
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:43,000
Responsibility, autonomy, and authority, they're all tied together.

70
00:05:43,979 --> 00:05:50,679
As you note, Allen building patrols is hard in the absence of a strong model,

71
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:58,659
but we're not really aiming at an ideal as much as we are taking our scouts through a process.

72
00:05:58,660 --> 00:06:04,780
And that's one of the more important things that we can do as scout leaders.

73
00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:12,340
Well, in this podcast, in Scout Mastership in 7 Minutes or Less, we're going to talk about the concept of fun.

74
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:15,680
That should be fun.

75
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:20,220
And then we're going to answer another email.

76
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:22,560
There's plenty to do, so let's get started, shall we?

77
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:26,880
Scout Mastership in 7 Minutes or Less.

78
00:06:28,660 --> 00:06:40,280
You know, there's a part in the annual planning process where the scouts sit down in the patrol leaders council,

79
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:47,980
and they discuss what they did in the last year, and then they're kind of hammering out what they're going to do in the upcoming year.

80
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:50,480
And I hear this a lot.

81
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:52,340
Well, we want it to be fun.

82
00:06:52,820 --> 00:06:55,940
I hear this repeated often by adult and youth leadership in Scouting.

83
00:06:56,060 --> 00:06:57,260
Fun, fun, fun, fun, fun.

84
00:06:58,180 --> 00:07:02,800
As if scouts are going to leave in droves if what they're doing isn't fun.

85
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:07,560
But what kind of fun are they really looking for?

86
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:10,680
That's a pretty good question.

87
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:16,460
I contend that boys are in Scouting not because they want more bread and circuses.

88
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:27,580
And bread and circuses are, that's an old saying, and it's meant to mean benefits or entertainments intended to placate discontent or to distract attention.

89
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:29,820
I don't think scouts want that.

90
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:31,720
They want an honest challenge.

91
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:33,300
They crave an honest challenge.

92
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:37,720
Listen, scouts love to learn, but they don't like to be educated.

93
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:41,340
They like hard work, but they don't like to be driven.

94
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:50,360
And they like to achieve, but they really don't like this kind of regimentation that is implied by achievement sometimes.

95
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:52,760
Well, what is an honest challenge?

96
00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:57,460
Obviously, it's not a canned experience leading to some predictable outcome.

97
00:07:58,780 --> 00:08:02,320
An honest challenge is kind of an open-ended idea.

98
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:08,600
And part of the honest challenge means that failure or disappointment are possible.

99
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:12,680
These aren't dangerous or capricious failures or disappointments.

100
00:08:12,820 --> 00:08:16,380
It's always safe and measured, but honest.

101
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:19,980
Boys are always going to find fun in meeting an honest challenge.

102
00:08:19,980 --> 00:08:25,020
They aren't looking for another source of entertainment or another program of activities.

103
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:29,780
They'll stay in scouts as long as the integrity of the challenge is maintained.

104
00:08:29,780 --> 00:08:39,220
If they sense that we're simply trying to entertain them, they're probably not going to stay alone long because, to tell you the truth, we're not all that entertaining.

105
00:08:39,699 --> 00:08:41,839
There's a lot better entertainment out there.

106
00:08:42,839 --> 00:08:44,319
So what exactly is an honest challenge?

107
00:08:44,459 --> 00:08:47,339
Well, the heart of it, the heart of it, guess what?

108
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:54,839
No big surprise here, is leading your peers with real responsibility and real authority.

109
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:56,580
That's a huge challenge.

110
00:08:56,780 --> 00:09:01,260
And there's many ways that you could be disappointed and there's many ways that you could fail.

111
00:09:02,220 --> 00:09:06,960
But boys can be very successful in doing that and they love that challenge.

112
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:09,460
This is what keeps older boys involved in scouting.

113
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:13,520
This is what is at the heart of the whole thing.

114
00:09:14,120 --> 00:09:22,820
This is not, you know, I mean, of course, going rappelling and, you know, doing all these fascinating outdoor activities and things like that.

115
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:24,140
Of course, that's part of it.

116
00:09:24,460 --> 00:09:28,600
But the real heart of it is this honest challenge of leadership.

117
00:09:29,540 --> 00:09:40,760
Of course, there's always the mental, physical, spiritual challenge of, like, putting a pack on your back and hiking into the woods and spending a couple nights out there with only those things that you have in your backpack.

118
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:44,700
And the skills that you have been taught or you have learned along the way.

119
00:09:44,780 --> 00:09:45,860
That's a great challenge.

120
00:09:47,260 --> 00:09:50,680
You know, getting into a canoe and paddling for a week through the wilderness.

121
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:52,440
What a wonderful challenge.

122
00:09:52,820 --> 00:09:54,540
How about this?

123
00:09:54,580 --> 00:10:03,360
When you're 11, you have to plan a menu and go and buy food and then prepare it for your fellow scouts.

124
00:10:03,480 --> 00:10:07,240
I mean, that's a huge challenge for somebody who's 11 years old.

125
00:10:08,680 --> 00:10:13,460
Even just getting dropped off 20 yards from the road to set up a tent and go camping for the weekend.

126
00:10:13,660 --> 00:10:14,480
Big challenge.

127
00:10:15,380 --> 00:10:17,480
Those are the honest challenges that I'm talking about.

128
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:19,020
Those are the things that we want to preserve.

129
00:10:19,020 --> 00:10:22,040
And those are the things that are at the heart of scouting.

130
00:10:22,600 --> 00:10:25,800
And keep a scout involved and engaged in it.

131
00:10:26,060 --> 00:10:29,860
And achieve the goals that we're looking for as scout leaders.

132
00:10:29,860 --> 00:10:47,780
making sure to see how important it is to play.

133
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:48,960
On the stretch.

134
00:10:49,180 --> 00:10:50,160
We'll be right back.

135
00:10:50,160 --> 00:11:08,000
Write me a letter.

136
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:10,219
Send it by mail.

137
00:11:11,819 --> 00:11:13,360
Email, that is, folks.

138
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:16,959
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

139
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:26,500
Well, here's a couple of emails that came in the past week.

140
00:11:26,699 --> 00:11:30,079
Here's one that says, I wanted to get your take on a problem I'm facing.

141
00:11:30,620 --> 00:11:37,040
I'm a Weebelos den leader, and we're looking towards bridging our boys into scouts next year.

142
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:41,620
But I have one boy who's having a bit of a rough time.

143
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:46,040
He's missed meetings, and I called his home to find out what's been happening,

144
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:48,000
and there are some major upheavals in the family.

145
00:11:48,780 --> 00:11:53,400
He's begun acting out, often refusing to go to meetings and therefore missing them.

146
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:57,480
His mother is at her wit's end on how to deal with his behavior,

147
00:11:57,740 --> 00:12:00,040
and was thinking of pulling him out of scouts.

148
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:02,920
In talking to his mother, I extolled the benefits of scouting

149
00:12:02,920 --> 00:12:06,080
and how it might help them through this rough patch.

150
00:12:07,020 --> 00:12:07,940
My question is this.

151
00:12:07,940 --> 00:12:10,520
Am I doing a good thing by encouraging the boy to stay in scouting,

152
00:12:10,660 --> 00:12:15,440
or am I just going to create a headache for the scoutmaster of the troop if he decides to join?

153
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:17,580
First of all, I appreciate you getting in touch.

154
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:19,780
Secondly, you're certainly doing a good thing.

155
00:12:20,660 --> 00:12:23,960
And possibly you're creating a headache for a scoutmaster at the same time.

156
00:12:24,040 --> 00:12:27,980
I mean, you would be surprised how often good things are a bit of a headache for scoutmasters.

157
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:32,220
I mean, we're scoutmasters because we want to help boys,

158
00:12:32,220 --> 00:12:38,400
and we need to accept them as they are and do whatever we can to see that their experience in scouting benefits them.

159
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:43,439
And every boy has behavior issues of one kind or another during his years as a scout.

160
00:12:43,920 --> 00:12:47,360
Scouting can be a refuge for boys whose families are having difficulties.

161
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:50,660
And what you describe isn't really all that unusual.

162
00:12:50,920 --> 00:12:52,280
Boys act out in many ways.

163
00:12:52,520 --> 00:12:55,420
Some very predictable ways, some less so.

164
00:12:55,980 --> 00:12:57,800
In the end, it's good that you care.

165
00:12:58,439 --> 00:13:02,900
And you're trying, and you're working with a family that's in a bit of a crisis.

166
00:13:02,900 --> 00:13:04,880
And do what you can.

167
00:13:05,520 --> 00:13:07,459
Often, there's not much that we can do.

168
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:14,520
But I'm sure that whatever support you're able to lend them is appreciated.

169
00:13:15,540 --> 00:13:17,020
Here's another email.

170
00:13:17,939 --> 00:13:20,280
Clark, we have a troop of about 32 boys.

171
00:13:20,760 --> 00:13:24,560
At most events, campouts, meetings, community service, Eagle Projects,

172
00:13:24,560 --> 00:13:27,640
we have a turnout of about 40% or less, which is frustrating.

173
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:32,160
Someone told me in the old days, a boy either played baseball or was a scout.

174
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:36,240
Now, with all of the sports and outside activities and school requirements,

175
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:39,540
the boys just don't seem to have the time to participate at a higher level.

176
00:13:40,319 --> 00:13:43,699
It's even difficult to get a call back from the senior patrol leader some weeks.

177
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:47,780
They have little time to participate, let alone spend the time it takes to plan,

178
00:13:47,920 --> 00:13:50,800
communicate, and follow up on planning campouts and events.

179
00:13:51,380 --> 00:13:52,760
Have you any guidance or insight?

180
00:13:53,620 --> 00:13:56,740
Would you not advance a scout based on attendance and participation,

181
00:13:57,060 --> 00:13:59,880
even if they technically meet all of the other requirements?

182
00:13:59,880 --> 00:14:00,080
Thanks.

183
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:01,160
Thanks.

184
00:14:01,700 --> 00:14:04,140
And that email came from David.

185
00:14:04,400 --> 00:14:06,939
David, I got to tell you, this is a pretty perennial complaint

186
00:14:06,939 --> 00:14:11,000
of just about anyone who has ever been a scoutmaster,

187
00:14:11,180 --> 00:14:14,780
starting it, you know, from 1910,

188
00:14:15,180 --> 00:14:18,780
when the Boy Scouts of America was founded, until the present day.

189
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:23,860
We chartered a bus to take our scouts backpacking last weekend,

190
00:14:23,860 --> 00:14:26,260
and we took nine scouts out of 40.

191
00:14:28,020 --> 00:14:29,720
Talk about frustrating, right?

192
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:33,060
After 26 years as a scoutmaster, I still haven't really figured out

193
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:35,380
why scouts attend or don't attend a given outing.

194
00:14:35,940 --> 00:14:39,140
I don't buy the theory that in the old days things were much different

195
00:14:39,140 --> 00:14:42,020
because I was there in the old days, and I can tell you that they weren't.

196
00:14:42,280 --> 00:14:44,100
I still ask myself these questions.

197
00:14:45,020 --> 00:14:47,940
You know, did they decide to stay home because they don't like scouts?

198
00:14:48,020 --> 00:14:51,180
Did they have other things that they thought were more important or more fun?

199
00:14:51,180 --> 00:14:53,579
You know, what am I doing wrong?

200
00:14:54,780 --> 00:14:58,400
Well, here's my take about our low turnout on our backpacking trip.

201
00:14:58,719 --> 00:15:02,300
I would say about five or six of our scouts were somehow involved in football,

202
00:15:02,620 --> 00:15:04,839
and we just happened to choose one of those weekends

203
00:15:04,839 --> 00:15:06,939
when there was a lot of football going on.

204
00:15:07,459 --> 00:15:09,520
Ten had some kind of family commitment.

205
00:15:10,420 --> 00:15:12,640
Two were nursing an injury and just couldn't go,

206
00:15:12,939 --> 00:15:16,680
and another got sick the day that we were getting ready to leave.

207
00:15:17,459 --> 00:15:19,199
Two had some kind of other activity,

208
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:21,560
you know, sports or something like that.

209
00:15:22,060 --> 00:15:23,400
And so if you add all that up,

210
00:15:23,540 --> 00:15:27,000
that accounts for 20 of my missing scouts.

211
00:15:27,460 --> 00:15:28,840
Add the nine that did attend,

212
00:15:28,920 --> 00:15:31,340
and that leaves 11 that I really can't explain,

213
00:15:31,520 --> 00:15:33,060
and I wouldn't expect to be able to.

214
00:15:33,500 --> 00:15:35,400
That's 11 left out of 40.

215
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:38,380
If I had asked each of these 11 why they didn't go,

216
00:15:38,460 --> 00:15:41,200
I would probably find that five had some good reason,

217
00:15:41,280 --> 00:15:43,840
and six simply didn't want to go and found a reason not to.

218
00:15:45,200 --> 00:15:48,680
This was really an anomaly over past backpacking trips.

219
00:15:48,680 --> 00:15:50,900
I mean, we usually have a great turnout at these outings,

220
00:15:50,959 --> 00:15:54,459
like 20 to 30 scouts and lots of adults.

221
00:15:54,719 --> 00:15:57,060
But I don't think we've ever had 100%.

222
00:15:57,060 --> 00:16:00,239
I don't think we've ever had 100% at any outing.

223
00:16:00,699 --> 00:16:03,839
We also have a good showing on our other outings this fall,

224
00:16:04,079 --> 00:16:07,000
and I just kind of have to mark this one up

225
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,839
as being a confluence of uncontrollable circumstances,

226
00:16:10,839 --> 00:16:12,420
and it led to a low turnout.

227
00:16:12,420 --> 00:16:14,660
Looking at it this way,

228
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:17,260
you find that you don't have a big problem.

229
00:16:18,140 --> 00:16:20,780
You just have the same background level of frustration

230
00:16:20,780 --> 00:16:22,699
we all encounter from time to time.

231
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:24,800
You know, you want to ask yourself these questions.

232
00:16:24,939 --> 00:16:26,300
Are my youth leaders really leading,

233
00:16:26,439 --> 00:16:28,280
or just following the plans I set up for them

234
00:16:28,280 --> 00:16:31,280
and attending the meetings I plan and present?

235
00:16:31,979 --> 00:16:33,900
Is the program a decision of the scouts?

236
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:35,439
Do they have real patrols,

237
00:16:35,920 --> 00:16:38,180
and have a real patrol leaders council

238
00:16:38,180 --> 00:16:40,640
that's active and engaged in every aspect of leadership?

239
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:43,780
I mean, those would be some key reasons

240
00:16:43,780 --> 00:16:46,780
that boys might not be participating

241
00:16:46,780 --> 00:16:48,900
at the level that you would expect them to.

242
00:16:49,579 --> 00:16:50,760
You note that your youth leaders

243
00:16:50,760 --> 00:16:52,239
aren't devoting a lot of time to leading,

244
00:16:52,319 --> 00:16:53,380
if I read things right.

245
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:54,420
Now, if they aren't,

246
00:16:54,459 --> 00:16:56,579
it may be because somebody else is doing it for them.

247
00:16:57,260 --> 00:16:59,140
If no one else does the leading,

248
00:16:59,260 --> 00:17:00,719
then they really have to do it.

249
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:02,300
That's a scary proposition,

250
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:04,859
but sometimes we have to stop planning and leading

251
00:17:04,859 --> 00:17:06,700
for our youth leaders to be able to step up

252
00:17:06,700 --> 00:17:07,500
and take the reins.

253
00:17:08,180 --> 00:17:10,700
As for evaluating active tenure,

254
00:17:11,380 --> 00:17:13,920
we've got really very specific language

255
00:17:13,920 --> 00:17:14,760
that guides us,

256
00:17:14,920 --> 00:17:16,120
and it says this,

257
00:17:16,160 --> 00:17:18,740
and you'll find this in several different resources,

258
00:17:19,060 --> 00:17:22,360
but most notably the advancement guide for 2010.

259
00:17:24,560 --> 00:17:26,960
A scout is considered to be active in his unit

260
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:28,440
if he's registered in his unit.

261
00:17:29,080 --> 00:17:30,940
He has not been dismissed for his unit

262
00:17:30,940 --> 00:17:32,500
for disciplinary reasons.

263
00:17:32,700 --> 00:17:34,440
He's engaged by his unit leadership

264
00:17:34,440 --> 00:17:35,580
on a regular basis,

265
00:17:35,580 --> 00:17:38,240
and that can mean a scoutmaster conference

266
00:17:38,240 --> 00:17:41,419
and that people inform them

267
00:17:41,419 --> 00:17:43,000
of upcoming unit activities

268
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,899
through personal contact and emails and whatever.

269
00:17:45,900 --> 00:17:52,440
those are the things that define being active,

270
00:17:52,640 --> 00:17:55,840
and we are not to add or take away from that standard.

271
00:17:56,500 --> 00:17:57,760
So at a scoutmaster's conference,

272
00:17:58,240 --> 00:18:01,440
when it comes to that requirement of a rank

273
00:18:01,440 --> 00:18:03,420
that says be active in your troop and patrol,

274
00:18:03,420 --> 00:18:07,260
I ask the scout to go ahead and evaluate himself.

275
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:09,160
I mean,

276
00:18:09,240 --> 00:18:11,540
how well do you think you've fulfilled that requirement?

277
00:18:12,060 --> 00:18:13,180
Do you think you've been active?

278
00:18:14,220 --> 00:18:16,780
And they are usually a little harder on themselves

279
00:18:16,780 --> 00:18:17,500
than I would be.

280
00:18:18,020 --> 00:18:18,420
David,

281
